course: dictionary
lesson: 15004
title: Feeling resent
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When something feels unfair, you may feel angry and hurt about it, and the feeling can stay for a long time. The word for this is resent. «Mia resented having to tidy her room while her brother played.» She was angry and hurt because she thought it was unfair.
explain: You can also resent a person for what they did or have. «Sam still resents Jake for breaking his model ship.» Sam is not just cross for a minute. The feeling stays with him, like a small stone in his pocket.
explain: In stories, resent often shows a hidden feeling. A character may not say it out loud, but you can spot it in what they do and say. When you read a word like resent, ask yourself: what has happened to make this person feel hurt?
fact: Resent is a verb: I resent, he resents, they resented, resenting.
fact: «Resent» means to feel angry and hurt about something you think is unfair, often for a long time.
fact: You can resent a person for what they did or have, as in «resent someone for».
fact: Phrases to notice: «resent being told», «deeply resent».
fact: «Re-sent» with a hyphen is the past of «resend», meaning sent again, as in «The teacher re-sent the letter to every family».
exercise: «Mia resented having to tidy her room while her brother played.» What does resented mean here? | Sent it again | Felt happy and proud of her room | Forgot about it quickly | *Felt angry and hurt about something unfair
exercise: «The teacher re-sent the letter to every family.» What does re-sent mean? | Read the letter aloud | Lost the letter for good | *Sent again | Felt angry about the letter
exercise: «Sam still resents Jake for breaking his model ship.» What does Sam feel towards Jake? | Sorry and eager to help | *Angry and hurt, and it has lasted a long time | Bored of playing games | Surprised by a birthday present
test: «Ben resents being told what to do.» Why might Ben feel this way? | He thinks being told what to do is funny | He is glad to be given instructions | He has never been told anything | *He thinks being told what to do is unfair
test: Which sentence uses resent in the sense of «sent again»? | *The office re-sent the bill after the first one got lost | The office resented the bill for a long time | The office paid the bill with a smile | The office was angry about the bill
exam: What does it mean to resent someone? | To send something to them again | To forget what they did quickly | *To feel angry and hurt about something unfair they did, often for a long time | To feel very happy for them
